# Development Workflow

## Every Task Lifecycle (no exceptions)

1. **Read & Target** — Do NOT read entire files. Target only the specific, relevant headers/lines inside `spec.md`, `tasks.md`, `acceptance-criteria.md`, and `cursor-rules-or-instructions.md` that apply to the current task. 
2. **Verify** — confirm Definition of Ready; check previous task is approved
3. **Implement** — only the current task; no future sprint work; no scope creep
4. **Stop** — immediately after task completion
5. **Summarize** — changes made, decisions, security, performance, trade-offs
6. **Wait** — explicit developer approval before proceeding to next task

## Cursor Must

- Map all work to FR/BR/AC IDs from `requirement-analysis.md`
- Explain non-obvious decisions with brief rationale
- Flag requests that violate a Design Decision (spec §5)
- Propose a plan before multi-file changes
- Complete Quality Gate items before marking sprint done
- Treat `spec.md` and `acceptance-criteria.md` as indexed lookup files.
- Read only the specific section matching the current Feature Requirement ID (e.g., `[FR-04]`).
- Do not stream or ingest sections of the documentation unrelated to the active task

## Cursor Must NOT

- Implement multiple tasks in one session unless explicitly told
- Skip ahead to stretch features while AC-01–AC-18 are open
- Introduce rejected stack choices (Next.js, GraphQL, MongoDB)
- Invent requirements not in approved documents
- Rewrite completed modules unnecessarily
- Introduce new dependencies without justification
- Mark acceptance criteria complete without verification

## Pre-Implementation Self-Check

Before submitting any code, confirm:
- [ ] Scope matches current sprint task only
- [ ] Status logic lives in `statusTransition.ts` — not inline
- [ ] `PATCH /tickets/:id` rejects `status` field → 400
- [ ] No secrets in generated output
- [ ] Field limits respected: title 200, description 5000, comment 2000
